"""Alan Kay's Dynabook sketch, traced: two children with their machines.

The 1972 drawing that put the thing in front of people before it could be
built — two kids sitting in the grass, each with a slab of glass in their lap.
This is that drawing and nothing else: the scan was run through a vectoriser,
and what is below is the outlines it found, replayed as dex paths.

It is an *image-style* node, not a canvas backdrop: it authors the drawing in
the tracer's own coordinate space and maps that into whatever box it is handed
— uniform scale, centred, letterboxed — so it keeps its shape at any size, the
way an image does, rather than stretching or ruling a whole plane.

Two things are worth knowing about the trace itself.

The ink is stored as SVG path data, verbatim, and parsed at load. That keeps the
drawing auditable — it is the tracer's output, not a hand-copy of it — and it
keeps the *curves*: a path carries Bezier handles and flattens them at the size
it is actually drawn, so the outlines stay smooth however far this is zoomed in.
Pre-flattening to points here would bake in one resolution.

And a filled outline has holes: the whites of the eyes, the gap inside a wrist.
SVG says which by winding, which a dex path has no notion of — it fills the one
loop it is given. So the loops are sorted out here instead, by asking how many
other loops each one is inside. Even depth is ink, odd depth is paper, and they
are painted outermost-first. Two colours and a containment test is the whole of
what a fill rule was doing.
"""

